    "created_at": "2026-06-30T16:29:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-30T16:29:41Z",
    "body": "I searched for use cases and wanted to document a few categories to expand on what is described in https://github.com/WICG/document-picture-in-picture.\n\nThe draft already mentions custom video players. Considering TikTok's floating player, there are several custom actions like bookmark, like, share, or view profile and together with a text description, they are laid out as on the regular web page.\n\nVideo conferencing was also mentioned. Google Meet makes use of the API to show multiple video streams, but also provides controls for each stream and a small control bar when screen sharing. Therefore, especially for video conferencing, it's important to control which area of the PiP can be interacted with.\n\nPomodoro timers are the third use case mentioned in the draft; I would categorize these as status widgets together with the Spotify mini player or a live ticker for sports (e.g. https://www.terra.com.br/). See also https://www.timekeep.cc/.\n\nAnother category is task widgets that provide controls while the user moves across pages. From HTTP Archive, I found a user research UI by Lyssna, a chat widget by LiveChat.com, HTML forms by JotForm.com and a bug submission tool by Jam.dev.\n\nLastly, there are some examples of pinned workspaces: https://notepad.js.org/, https://blinkpad.netlify.app/, the live preview for a web page editor described in https://developer.chrome.com/blog/document-pip-use-case, and MDN documentation kept always-on-top as shown by an extension (https://github.com/boryssey/picture-in-picture-anything).",
